1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,980
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 8, The Case for Illusion. Right now, without moving your eyes, ask yourself how much of what you see is in color. For almost everyone, the answer comes back instantly. All of it.

2
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:22,160
In 2020, the psychologists Michael Cohen, Thomas Botch, and Caroline Robertson tested that confidence.

3
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:27,900
Participants wore virtual reality headsets with eye-tracking and explored immersive scenes.

4
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:31,960
A historic site, a street dance, an orchestra rehearsal.

5
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:37,080
The software kept color only where each person was looking and drained it from everything else,

6
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:39,200
turning the periphery black and white.

7
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:41,480
Most participants never noticed.

8
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:46,660
In the most extreme condition, with color left in only a small central patch,

9
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:51,740
less than 5% of the visual field, nearly a third of them still did not notice.

10
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:56,500
That's a fact about peripheral vision. Nobody thinks the eye is perfect.

11
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,960
The eye is not what failed, though. What failed was the report.

12
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:04,820
People were asked whether anything had changed in what they saw,

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,820
and most reported nothing amiss, although much of their visual field had lost its color.

14
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:16,400
read that. The realist reading says the brain filled in the color, so the experience really

15
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:22,000
was colored, and introspection got the experience right while the world was different. The other

16
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,320
reading says people's judgments about their experience ran ahead of anything going on.

17
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:32,880
Either way, the sense of a rich, uniform, colorful visual field turns out to be a construction,

18
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:38,320
and introspection did not flag it as one. Today's question is how far that goes.

19
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,360
Could introspection be wrong not just about the details of experience,

20
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:47,760
but about its most basic character, the very thing that makes the hard problem seem hard?

21
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:53,080
That is the claim of illusionism, and today it gets the strongest hearing I can give it,

22
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,300
followed by its hardest objections.

23
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:00,820
Start with what the view actually says, because it's routinely caricatured.

24
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:04,780
The modern statement is Keith Frankish's 2016 paper,

25
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:11,100
illusionism as a theory of consciousness published as a target article in the journal of consciousness

26
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:17,480
studies with a symposium of replies later collected in a book frankish draws a line

27
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:22,860
between two things that ordinary talk runs together on one side is consciousness in the

28
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:28,660
everyday functional sense you're awake you see the tomato you can attend to it think about it

29
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:34,700
report it. When you stub your toe, a state arises that registers damage, seizes your attention,

30
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:40,860
and makes you want it to stop. Illusionists deny none of this. On the other side is phenomenal

31
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:46,920
consciousness in the philosopher's sense. Special qualities of experience, qualia, that are supposed

32
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:53,600
to be intrinsic, private, ineffable, and known directly. The redness of the red, considered as

33
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:59,900
something over and above everything the experience does. That is what illusionism denies. Frankish's

34
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:04,680
claim is that there are no such phenomenal properties. What exist are what he calls

35
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:10,740
quasi-phenomenal properties. Physical properties of brain states that introspection represents as

36
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:16,820
having that special phenomenal character. The representation is real. The character it attributes

37
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:22,540
is not. So what am I supposed to say about a toothache, that it only seems to hurt?

38
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:28,620
It hurts. Everything that hurting does is real. The alarm, the distraction, the misery,

39
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:34,720
the plans to see a dentist. What the illusionist denies is a further private glow that floats free

40
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:39,380
of all that, which introspection seems to point at when you attend to the pain in a philosophical

41
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:46,760
mood. The hurting is real. The extra, ineffable quality is the illusion. Frankish also distinguishes

42
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:52,900
strong illusionism from weak. Weak illusionism says phenomenal properties exist but are not

43
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:58,720
quite as they seem. He argues that position is unstable because once you strip away the strange

44
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:04,200
features, what's left is functional and representational anyway. So he defends the

45
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:09,720
strong version. And he insists on a positive research program, which he calls the illusion

46
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:15,180
problem. Explain why introspection represents our states as having phenomenal properties

47
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:23,680
is so compelling. Compelling is the key word. In the Müller-Leyer illusion, two equal lines with

48
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:29,640
arrowheads pointing in or out look unequal even after you've measured them. Knowing the explanation

49
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:35,300
does not switch an illusion off. So if illusionism is true, your sense of an inner glow should

50
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:41,400
survive your accepting the theory, and its stubborn obviousness is not, by itself, evidence against

51
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:48,840
the view. The view's deepest roots are in Daniel Dennett, who died in 2024 and who spent half a

52
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:55,700
century on this project. In a 1988 paper, Quining Qualia, Dennett went after the very concept of

53
00:04:55,700 --> 00:05:01,820
qualia as philosophers define it. His method was the intuition pump, a carefully designed story

54
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:07,580
that shows you how your intuitions work. The best known involves two professional coffee tasters

55
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:13,320
at the same company. Both have tasted the house blend for years, and both have stopped liking it.

56
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:19,000
One says the coffee tastes the same to him, but his standards have changed. The other says his

57
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,300
taste buds must have changed, so the coffee now tastes different to him. Dennett pressed the

58
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:29,540
question, what could possibly settle which of them is right? Perhaps there is no fact of the matter

59
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:35,860
at all, and if a concept generates questions with no possible answers, maybe the concept is confused.

60
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:43,120
book, Consciousness Explained, attacked a picture he called the Cartesian Theater. The idea that

61
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:47,560
somewhere in the brain there's a place where it all comes together, a screen watched by an inner

62
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:53,280
audience, and that a content becomes conscious at the moment it arrives there. The brain, he argued,

63
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:59,380
has no such place and no such moment. What it is are multiple drafts, parallel streams of content,

64
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:07,020
constantly edited and revised. His favorite example was ColorFi. In 1976, Paul Kohlers and

65
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:14,480
Grunau flashed a red dot and then quickly, a green dot a short distance away. People see one dot

66
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:20,180
moving and turning green partway along its path before the green dot has even appeared. So how

67
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:25,560
did the brain know? Either it held the experience back and edited it before release, which Dennett

68
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:30,560
called the Stalin-esque story, or you saw it correctly and your memory was rewritten, the

69
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:36,120
Orwellian story. Dennett's provocative conclusion was that there is no fact about which is true,

70
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:48,320
A distributed system has no global clock either, and things still happen in it.

71
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:03,460
Things happen in it all the time.

72
00:07:04,020 --> 00:07:08,120
What's missing is a single moment when one of them officially becomes experience.

73
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:11,780
In place of the theater, Dennett offered a method and a slogan.

74
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:14,340
The method was heterophenomenology.

75
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:18,100
Take people's reports about their experience with total seriousness,

76
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,560
as data about how things seem to them,

77
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:24,560
without assuming the reports are accurate descriptions of what's happening inside.

78
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,100
The slogan was fame in the brain.

79
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:30,900
A content becomes conscious the way a person becomes famous,

80
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:35,680
by winning influence over the rest of the system, not by entering a special room.

81
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:39,160
And he borrowed an image from computing, the user illusion.

82
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,380
Your laptop shows you folders and a trash can.

83
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:47,220
There are no tiny folders on the chip, but the interface is real, useful,

84
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:49,960
and the only way you ever deal with what's underneath.

85
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:54,500
On this view, the felt inner world is the brain's interface to itself.

86
00:07:56,160 --> 00:08:00,340
Illusionism needs evidence that introspection can be wrong about our own minds.

87
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:06,880
There's a lot of it. In 1977, Richard Nisbet and Timothy Wilson published a classic paper

88
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:12,600
with the title telling more than we can know. In one study, shoppers choosing among identical

89
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:18,640
items laid out in a row tended to pick the one on the right. Asked why, they cited quality,

90
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:23,880
and denied that position had mattered. People confidently report the causes of their own

91
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:30,240
choices and are often wrong. The philosopher Eric S. H. Witzgabel has argued that the problem goes

92
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:37,120
to experience itself. In a 2008 paper on the unreliability of naive introspection,

93
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:42,420
he collected basic questions about current experience on which careful people disagree.

94
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:47,860
How clear the periphery of the visual field is, whether emotions have a bodily feel,

95
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:53,280
whether thought has a sensory character. These are questions about what experience is like right now,

96
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:59,500
and introspection gives conflicting answers. The psychologist Russell Hurlburt has spent decades

97
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:06,840
descriptive experience sampling. A beeper goes off at random moments, and you describe exactly

98
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:12,560
what was in your experience just before it. In one study of 30 people, inner speech showed up

99
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:18,100
in about a quarter of samples on average, but with enormous variation. Some people reported

100
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:24,280
none at all, others three quarters of the time. Hurlburt's long-running conclusion is that people's

101
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:29,700
armchair generalizations about their own inner lives are far less trustworthy than careful

102
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:38,120
moments. And in 2015, the neurologist Adam Zemmen gave a name to a fantasia, the lifelong absence of

103
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:43,420
voluntary visual imagery. Some people discover only as adults that when others say they can

104
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:48,520
picture a face, they mean it literally. A difference as large as having inner pictures

105
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:54,560
or not can go unnoticed for decades because everyone uses the same words. In episode 10,

106
00:09:54,860 --> 00:10:00,620
we'll add choice blindness to this file. All of that shows introspection is unreliable about

107
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:05,240
It does not show it's wrong about there being experience at all.

108
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:09,600
You're sliding from sometimes mistaken to mistaken about everything.

109
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:14,240
That's exactly the right objection, and the illusionist has to answer it.

110
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:17,900
Local unreliability does not establish global error.

111
00:10:18,180 --> 00:10:20,640
What illusionists add is a second step.

112
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:25,760
They argue that we can explain why we'd believe in phenomenal properties even if there were none,

113
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:32,940
and that if such an explanation works, believing in them anyway is like believing in a ghost after you've found the projector.

114
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:35,100
Which brings us to the metaproblem.

115
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:39,400
Last episode ended with Chalmers' metaproblem.

116
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:41,580
Why do we think there is a hard problem?

117
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:48,020
His 2018 paper, which drew a large set of replies, calls the culprit's problem intuitions.

118
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:49,840
He sorts them into families.

119
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:51,880
Explanatory intuitions.

120
00:10:52,180 --> 00:10:54,400
Experience cannot be explained physically.

121
00:10:55,760 --> 00:11:02,000
intuitions, experience is not physical. Knowledge intuitions, like Mary's, modal intuitions,

122
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:08,000
like zombies, and these intuitions run deep. The developmental psychologist Paul Bloom has

123
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:13,340
argued that children are natural dualists, readily treating minds as separable from bodies.

124
00:11:13,660 --> 00:11:19,080
What might the answer look like? The answers I find most promising involve introspective models.

125
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:25,160
The brain represents its own perceptual states in a simplified way, as primitive qualities with

126
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:31,700
structure because it has no need to represent the underlying machinery. A system with a model like

127
00:11:31,700 --> 00:11:37,560
that would find its own states ineffable, intrinsic, and impossible to derive from anything physical

128
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:43,400
because the model offers nothing to derive them from. Then comes the twist that makes the metaproblem

129
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:49,860
dangerous for realists. Suppose we find a complete physical explanation of why we say there's a hard

130
00:11:49,860 --> 00:11:55,960
problem, and that explanation never needs to mention experience itself. Then our beliefs about

131
00:11:55,960 --> 00:12:01,280
experience would be explained without experience playing any role. That threatens to debunk them,

132
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:06,580
the way learning that a belief was produced by a process indifferent to its truth undermines the

133
00:12:06,580 --> 00:12:13,160
belief. Chalmers takes this seriously. Either the illusionist is right, or the realist must show

134
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:19,080
that experience itself plays some role in producing our talk about it. That's why he treats illusionism

135
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:26,720
rival to his own view, not as a fringe position. Here's what bugs me. Any language model trained

136
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:33,300
on human text will reproduce problem intuitions. Ask one about its experience, and it may tell you

137
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:39,760
its inner life is mysterious and hard to put into words. Does that make the metaproblem easier or

138
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:45,980
harder? Both, I think. Easier because it proves that problem intuition behavior can be produced

139
00:12:45,980 --> 00:12:51,780
by a mechanism we can inspect. Harder, because it shows the behavior can arise for reasons that

140
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:56,260
have nothing to do with having experience or even with having an introspective model,

141
00:12:56,500 --> 00:13:01,760
simply by learning from people who talk this way. In a language model, the reports alone

142
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:07,360
tell you very little. That turns out to be one of the central obstacles in episodes 12 and 13.

143
00:13:08,720 --> 00:13:14,100
Several researchers have tried to say what the mechanism actually is. The most concrete is

144
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:37,800
The brain constantly controls its own attention, and good control requires a model of the thing being controlled, the good regulator idea from episode 4. So the brain builds a simplified model of its attention, and that model depicts not neurons competing for signal strength, but something simpler. An inner essence that grasps things, an awareness.

145
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:43,660
When you say you're aware of the cup, Graziano argues, you're reporting the contents of that schema.

146
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:50,920
It's the closest thing illusionism has to a wiring diagram, though Graziano himself dislikes the word illusion.

147
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:56,900
Attention is real, he says, and awareness is the brain's useful caricature of it.

148
00:13:57,040 --> 00:14:00,580
In episode 11, we'll treat it as a scientific theory.

149
00:14:00,580 --> 00:14:10,240
In 2021, Andrew Wilterson and Graziano built a deep reinforcement learning agent that had to control a simulated spotlight of attention to catch a falling ball.

150
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:15,060
Given an attention schema, a model of its own attention, it learned the task.

151
00:14:15,420 --> 00:14:18,380
With the schema disabled, its performance collapsed.

152
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:21,360
That shows a functional reason to have such a model.

153
00:14:21,640 --> 00:14:27,380
It says nothing about whether the agent experiences anything, and Graziano does not claim it does.

154
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:31,520
Thomas Metzinger's Being No One argues that there is no such thing as a self.

155
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:36,300
What exists is a phenomenal self-model that the brain cannot recognize as a model.

156
00:14:36,660 --> 00:14:39,660
It's transparent, like a window you look through rather than at,

157
00:14:39,820 --> 00:14:43,100
so you experience yourself as a self rather than as a model of one.

158
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:48,160
Douglas Hofstadter's Gertl, Escher, Bach, and later I Am a Strange Loop,

159
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:50,320
argue that a self is a strange loop,

160
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:54,340
a pattern in a symbol-processing system that comes to represent itself.

161
00:14:54,760 --> 00:15:00,180
The psychologist Nicholas Humphrey is a fellow traveler rather than a card-carrying illusionist.

162
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:07,200
In Soul Dust and in Sentience, from 2022, he describes phenomenal consciousness as a kind of

163
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:13,380
magical show the brain stages for itself, one that may have evolved because it makes being alive feel

164
00:15:13,380 --> 00:15:18,820
worth the trouble. But in his reply to Frankish, he resisted the word illusion and called his view

165
00:15:18,820 --> 00:15:25,260
phenomenal surrealism. The show is constructed, he thinks, but it is not a mistake. It's the

166
00:15:25,260 --> 00:15:30,460
brain's own artwork, and it matters to us. And Jeffrey Hinton has offered a version aimed

167
00:15:30,460 --> 00:15:36,160
squarely at machines. In an interview with The Globe and Mail, he rejected the idea of an inner

168
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:41,900
theater. On his account, talk of subjective experience is a way of describing what your

169
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:47,200
perceptual system is telling you when it is not working properly. The state of the world that

170
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:54,560
to be right. Then he imagined a chatbot with a camera, trained to point at objects. Put a prism

171
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:59,820
in front of the lens, and it points to the wrong place. Tell it about the prism, and it explains

172
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:05,060
that the object is really straight ahead, but it had the subjective experience of it being off to

173
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:11,000
one side. Hinton's claim is that it uses the phrase exactly as we do, and so has subjective

174
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:16,900
experience just as we do. That seems too quick. The chatbot learned that phrase from us.

175
00:16:18,820 --> 00:16:23,860
the machine episodes we'll call the gaming problem. A system trained on our words can

176
00:16:23,860 --> 00:16:29,640
give our reports without our reasons. Blaise Aguera e Arcus, whom we met in episode four,

177
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:36,840
takes the same line as Graziano. Attention is a real computation, he argues, so a model of it is

178
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:43,180
no more an illusion than a chair is. Like Graziano, he accepts the mechanism and resists the name.

179
00:16:44,300 --> 00:16:50,680
Fiction got here early. Peter Watts' 2006 novel Blindsight, which drew openly on Metzinger,

180
00:16:51,180 --> 00:16:57,480
sends a crew of modified humans to meet aliens that are vastly capable and, it seems, entirely

181
00:16:57,480 --> 00:17:02,580
non-conscious and asks whether consciousness might be a costly side effect rather than the

182
00:17:02,580 --> 00:17:08,100
crown of intelligence. It's the doing-versus feeling split as a thriller, and Watts has made

183
00:17:08,100 --> 00:17:13,640
it free to read online under a Creative Commons license. Notice that the novel works under either

184
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:20,100
reading. A realist reads the aliens as lacking experience. An illusionist reads them as lacking

185
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:26,280
the self-model and therefore lacking the illusion. Episode 10 has the real-world counterpart,

186
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:30,600
patients with blindsight, who respond to what they say they cannot see.

187
00:17:31,700 --> 00:17:37,480
Now the objections at full strength. In 2018, on the New York Review of Books website,

188
00:17:38,100 --> 00:17:42,400
Philosopher Galen Strawson called the denial of consciousness the silliest claim ever made,

189
00:17:42,740 --> 00:17:44,020
aiming chiefly at Dennett.

190
00:17:44,440 --> 00:17:47,380
Dennett replied that Strawson had misdescribed his view.

191
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,780
Consciousness is real, he said.

192
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:52,300
It just is not what Strawson takes it to be.

193
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:55,480
Strawson's answer was that what illusionists deny,

194
00:17:55,740 --> 00:17:57,720
the qualitative character of experience,

195
00:17:58,020 --> 00:18:00,200
is exactly the thing we know for certain.

196
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:03,560
That's the core objection, and it can be put in one line.

197
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:07,760
An illusion is a way things seem, and a seeming is an experience,

198
00:18:08,100 --> 00:18:11,400
so illusionism presupposes the very thing it denies.

199
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:15,180
John Searle made the point that where consciousness is concerned,

200
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:17,080
the appearance is the reality.

201
00:18:17,440 --> 00:18:20,440
You can be wrong about whether there's a tomato in front of you.

202
00:18:20,660 --> 00:18:23,460
You cannot be wrong that it seems to you that there is.

203
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:27,440
Frankish's reply is that a seeming can be a representational state,

204
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:31,160
a kind of judgment-like state produced by introspective mechanisms,

205
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:33,520
without any extra phenomenal property.

206
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:36,980
Representations can misrepresent without themselves glowing.

207
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:53,460
Let me put that in my language.

208
00:18:53,920 --> 00:19:00,180
Illusionism says the brain's self-model is lossy and confidently wrong about its own internals.

209
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:01,720
That's not exotic.

210
00:19:02,100 --> 00:19:05,380
Every learned model of a complex system is lossy,

211
00:19:05,380 --> 00:19:09,140
and nothing forces a system's model of itself to be accurate.

212
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:14,020
That's a good translation, and it's why many engineers find the view natural.

213
00:19:14,220 --> 00:19:17,440
But the philosopher Philip Goff presses a different point.

214
00:19:18,140 --> 00:19:22,280
Consciousness is the one thing each of us knows more directly than anything else.

215
00:19:22,440 --> 00:19:24,960
All the evidence for any scientific theory,

216
00:19:25,260 --> 00:19:29,960
including the theory that experience is an illusion, reaches us through experience.

217
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:35,640
So illusionism carries a heavier burden of proof than any ordinary empirical theory.

218
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,620
It asks us to doubt the medium through which we know everything else.

219
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:43,700
And there's the illusion problem itself, which is not yet solved.

220
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:48,880
Why would evolution build a self-model that represents our states as ineffable and intrinsic?

221
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:52,340
Graziano says simplification serves control.

222
00:19:52,780 --> 00:19:55,460
Humphrey says the show makes life feel worth living.

223
00:19:56,060 --> 00:19:58,780
Dennett says the interface hides what you do not need to see.

224
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:06,500
stories, not finished explanations. A view that asks this much is easy to dismiss from outside.

225
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:13,000
The philosopher Joe Carl Smith's essay Grokking Illusionism is a sustained attempt to understand

226
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:17,640
it from the inside instead, and that is the right spirit in which to judge it.

227
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:23,900
Why does this view matter so much for this series? Because it changes the machine question

228
00:20:23,900 --> 00:20:29,560
more than any other position. If illusionism is right, then asking whether a machine is

229
00:20:29,960 --> 00:20:33,500
becomes asking whether it has the relevant kind of self-model.

230
00:20:34,280 --> 00:20:36,780
Introspective representations of its own states

231
00:20:36,780 --> 00:20:38,540
as having phenomenal qualities,

232
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:40,400
produced for the right reasons,

233
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:42,580
and driving what it says about itself.

234
00:20:42,580 --> 00:20:44,540
That's a question about mechanisms,

235
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:47,600
and interpretability research can, in principle,

236
00:20:48,060 --> 00:20:51,240
look for internal states that track the system's own processing

237
00:20:51,240 --> 00:20:52,800
and feed its self-reports.

238
00:20:53,340 --> 00:20:56,860
Episode 12 covers early experiments of that kind.

239
00:20:57,060 --> 00:20:58,360
But notice the trap.

240
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:04,980
trained on human writing can produce flawless reports of ineffable inner experience without

241
00:21:04,980 --> 00:21:11,380
any such self-model, just by learning how we talk. So illusionism does not make the machine question

242
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:17,340
easy. It makes it empirical. The test is not what the system says. It's why it says it.

243
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:24,120
In 2023, Dennett's own warning about AI was not about machines that feel. It was about machines

244
00:21:24,120 --> 00:21:29,560
that pass for people, which he called counterfeit people. We'll come back to that warning in the

245
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:37,720
episode. Score it for me, where does it stand? In its favor, parsimony, since it adds no new

246
00:21:37,720 --> 00:21:43,160
ingredient to the world. Tractability, since it turns the deepest question into the meta-problem,

247
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:49,100
which science can work on. And the track record of introspection, bad enough that trusting it

248
00:21:49,100 --> 00:21:55,500
on the biggest question of all needs an argument. Against it, the seeming objection, which illusionists

249
00:21:55,500 --> 00:22:01,060
have answered without silencing. The burden of proof, since it asks us to doubt what seems most

250
00:22:01,060 --> 00:22:07,240
certain. And the illusion problem, which remains a program rather than a result. What illusionism

251
00:22:07,240 --> 00:22:13,300
establishes, I think, is that the hard problem's most powerful intuitions could be the outputs of

252
00:22:13,300 --> 00:22:19,120
a mechanism. It does not establish that they are. That's a real achievement, and it's why the

253
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:24,120
meta-problem comes back in the final episode, as one of the things that could change minds.

254
00:22:25,500 --> 00:22:30,900
we lay out the whole map, every serious position on the hard problem, what each one costs,

255
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:36,660
and what each one says about octopuses and machines. And at the end, a short, clearly

256
00:22:36,660 --> 00:22:37,800
marked trip to the edges.

